  "query_preview": "..Broad high pressure currently sits over the\nSouthwest with Hurricane Rachel well to the south about 600\nmiles southwest of Cabo. Even with Rachel being so far away\ntropical moisture is still making its way into northern Mexico.\nPWATs in Sonora are around 1.5\" and this moisture is expected to\nslowly move northward today. However not expecting much in the\nway of storms, mainly buildups along the higher terrain. Hi-res\nguidance does show a weak gulf surge that may make it to the\nOrgan Pipe National Monument area. This could lead to an\nisolated storm or two in western Pima County. Otherwise the\neasterly pressure gradient is still in place today, although\nweaker than it was over the weekend. Expect breezy east winds\nthis morning into the early afternoon with speeds 12-18 mph and\ngusts 25-35 mph. \n\nHigh pressure hangs around for most of the work week keeping\ntemperatures around normal to a few degrees above normal.\nDeterministic models show a weak shortwave moving through\nArizona Wednesday which may kick off isolated storms over the\nWhite Mountains during the afternoon. NBM PoPs are currently\n15-30 percent. Starting Thursday another easterly surface\npressure gradient begins to set up leading to breezy east winds\nThursday into Saturday. \n\nThis weekend into early next week the forecast becomes more\nuncertain as a trough forms along the West Coast. Most ensemble\nmembers show a trough during this time period, but there is \nfairly large spread in the depth and strength of the trough. \nThis has implications for Rachel, which will be well off the \nwest coast of Baja by this weekend. A trough that extends \nfurther south could bring Sarah back towards Baja, which is what\nthe Euro is showing. After Friday there is lots of uncertainty \nin Sarah's track. For now the forecast is trending wetter with \n20-40 PoPs starting Sunday into Monday as the trough moves \nthrough. Temperatures also drop to around normal or a few \ndegrees below normal and winds will likely be breezy out of the \nsouthwest.",
